 ## Background
-The concept of optimizing camera poses alongside NeRF parameters was introduced in "NeRF--Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" by Mildenhall et al. (2020), which established the differentiable rendering pipeline. However, the specific approach of treating camera poses as learnable parameters without external SfM initialization was notably detailed in "NeRF--Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (specifically the "NeRF with Unknown Camera Parameters" variant) and further explored in works like "NeRF--Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" [S2, unofficial]. The method was developed to address the dependency on external Structure-from-Motion pipelines, which can fail on textureless or repetitive scenes. It has since been extended to 2D/3D registration in medical imaging [S1], monocular video reconstruction [S1][S2, unofficial], and robotic navigation [S1].
+The problem of optimizing camera poses jointly with NeRF parameters was first explicitly addressed in the context of unknown camera parameters by [NeRF with Unknown Camera Parameters](7405-nerf-with-unknown-camera-parameters.md), which demonstrated that joint optimization could achieve quality comparable to COLMAP-initialized NeRFs [S2, unofficial]. Subsequent work expanded this to include depth priors ([NoPe-NeRF](8705-nope-nerf.md), [AltNeRF](8763-altnerf.md)) and specific applications like medical imaging ([Hybrid 2D/3D Registration](8120-hybrid-2d-3d-registration-optimization-pipeline.md)) and robotics ([Vision-Only Pose Filtering](10501-vision-only-pose-filtering-in-a-nerf.md), [Online Receding Horizon Replanning](10509-online-receding-horizon-replanning.md), [NeRF-based Trajectory Optimization](10632-nerf-based-trajectory-optimization.md)). The technique of NeRF registration using Viewshed Fields was introduced in [VF-NeRF](S1).
 
